\f2\fs32 \cf3 Stuck Student Protocol
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs18 \cf4 Companion Reference Document  |  v0.1  |  June 2026  |  Internal Use Only
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa180\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf3 Document Status
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 This document specifies the Stuck Student protocol \'97 the secondary teaching approach that activates when the default Score Loop documented in LEARNING_SYSTEM.docx has failed for a particular skill. It is the natural companion to LEARNING_SYSTEM, named as out-of-scope in Section 11 of that document and developed here in full.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 LEARNING_SYSTEM remains the authority on the default teaching loop, the three Repair modes, calibration, student-supplied questions, and the data posture. This document is the authority on what Cramapple does when that loop is not working for a student on a specific skill. The two are mutually exclusive at the per-skill level: at any moment, for any given skill, a student is either in the Score Loop or in the Stuck protocol, never both.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Statements labeled Decision reflect the current direction. Statements labeled Hypothesis require testing. Items labeled Open remain unresolved.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs18 \cf4 Changelog. v0.1 (June 2026) initial draft. Establishes the three Stuck-protocol moves (Sideways, Apart, Down), the selection sequence (Sideways always first; student-choice between Apart and Down; both offered before parking), the Park exit, the soft-visibility UX commitment, the button-based choice prompt, and the preference-memory feature in the Lock.
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa180\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf3 1. Premise
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs22 \cf3 The Score Loop is built for the case where a student is progressing. It assumes the student has the foundational knowledge, can parse the prompt, can hold the task in working memory, and is willing to wrestle with feedback. When those conditions hold, the loop's intra-loop escalation (Tighten \uc0\u8594  Show on diagnostic confidence drop, per LEARNING_SYSTEM 4.4) reliably closes most gaps.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Some students miss the same skill three times in a row anyway. Some hold persistently low diagnostic confidence across attempts. When that happens, re-presenting the same content a fourth time does nothing useful. Cramapple has already tried the cheap and the more expensive Repair within the loop. Continuing to run the loop on this skill is friction without progress.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The Stuck protocol exists because the default loop's failure is itself a signal: the student needs something structurally different, not more of the same. The protocol's job is to pick a different intervention, run it, and either return the student to the Score Loop with the skill resolved or park the skill for later when neither intervention takes.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb120\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf2 Decision.
\f2\b0 \cf3  The Stuck protocol is a per-skill state, not a per-student or per-session state. A student can be stuck on chi-square interpretation while progressing normally on cellular respiration in the same session. Cramapple manages the state at the skill level.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs18 \cf4 This commitment matters operationally. It means the protocol does not stigmatize the student, does not pause the session, and does not require any global judgment about whether the student is "struggling." It is a targeted response to a specific skill where the loop is not working.
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa180\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf3 2. Entry Triggers
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Two triggers move a skill from the Score Loop into the Stuck protocol. Both are named in LEARNING_SYSTEM Section 11 and are restated here in operational form.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa100\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f5\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Three consecutive misses on the same skill. The student has attempted the skill three times \'97 typically across an initial encounter and two Lock re-encounters at 24\'9672 hour intervals \'97 and missed the point each time. Each attempt has run through the full Score Loop including intra-loop escalation from Tighten to Show where confidence was low.\
\ls1\ilvl0\ulnone \strike0\striked0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Persistently low diagnostic confidence. Across two or more attempts on the same skill, Cramapple's diagnostic step has been unable to classify the error type with sufficient confidence. The grading pipeline returns an error type but flags the rationale as weak, or the error type oscillates between attempts. This trigger fires earlier than the three-miss trigger and catches the case where the student is missing the skill in different ways each time \'97 itself a signal that the underlying problem is not a knowledge gap the loop can close.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb120\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0 \cf2 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 Decision.
\f2\b0 \cf3  The triggers are skill-specific and Lock-aware. Three failed attempts within a single sitting count, but so do three failed attempts spread across Lock re-encounters over a week. The Lock surfaces the previously-missed skill in a different surface form each time, so failures across Lock re-encounters are a stronger signal than failures in immediate succession.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs18 \cf4 Open: the exact threshold for "persistently low diagnostic confidence." The grading pipeline (LEARNING_SYSTEM 3.3) returns per-criterion rationales with confidence weights; the threshold for triggering the Stuck protocol on confidence rather than miss-count needs calibration on real student data. Default working assumption: two consecutive attempts where the highest-confidence error classification falls below 0.6.
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa180\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf3 3. The Three Moves
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The Score Loop has three Repair modes (Tighten, Show, Stretch) corresponding to three different shapes of within-loop intervention. The Stuck protocol has three moves of its own, each designed for the case where the loop itself has not worked. The moves are not Repair modes \'97 they replace the Score Loop for this skill rather than running inside it.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Each move stays grounded in the cognitive science literature, and each maps to a distinct hypothesis about why the loop has failed. The moves are not ordered by severity. They are ordered by cognitive distance from the original problem \'97 how far the student has to travel from the original task to engage with the move.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 3.1 Step Sideways
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Same skill, same level, different angle. The student encounters a contrasting case, a reframed version of the same concept, or a deliberately constructed example designed to make a likely misconception visible by contradiction.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 When it works. 
\f2\b0 The student's loop failure was driven by an interaction with the specific framing of the original problem \'97 a surface feature, a misread of the prompt, or an entrenched mental model that the original framing reinforced. Re-presenting the same problem fails because the same framing keeps triggering the same wrong response. A reframed version short-circuits the pattern.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 In practice. 
\f2\b0 For a chi-square problem the student keeps misinterpreting, Cramapple constructs a structurally identical problem in a different scientific context (e.g., shifting from plant pigmentation ratios to a Mendelian cross) and asks the student to attempt that. For a conceptual question the student keeps answering with a wrong mental model, Cramapple presents two response options side by side \'97 the student's reasoning and the correct reasoning \'97 and asks which is internally consistent with the prompt's evidence. The contrasting-case approach is the conceptual-change literature's preferred move.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 Source. 
\f2\b0 Driver et al. and Chi on conceptual change. The central finding is that re-presenting correct information rarely dislodges a held misconception; what works is forcing the student into cognitive dissonance and giving them the tools to resolve it. Step Sideways operationalizes that principle.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 3.2 Step Apart
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Same skill, broken into atomic sub-skills, each solved in isolation, then re-integrated. The student handles one component at a time before recomposing the full task.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 When it works. 
\f2\b0 The loop has failed because the integrated task overwhelms working memory or because the student's procedural execution fragments mid-task. Each individual step works in isolation but the student loses the thread when stringing them together. Decomposition removes the integration load.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 In practice. 
\f2\b0 For a multi-step quantitative FRQ the student keeps fumbling, Cramapple breaks the task into single-step sub-problems \'97 state the hypothesis, compute the expected value, compute the test statistic, compare to the critical value, state the conclusion \'97 and runs each as its own micro-attempt with its own confirmation. Only after each step is confirmed does Cramapple ask the student to recompose the full procedure on a parallel problem. For a complex experimental-analysis FRQ, Cramapple asks the student to identify the independent variable, then the dependent variable, then the control, then to construct the experimental claim \'97 separating the components before the synthesis.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 Source. 
\f2\b0 Sweller and the cognitive load theory literature. Element interactivity \'97 the number of pieces a student must hold simultaneously to complete a task \'97 is a known driver of failure on complex multi-step problems. Decomposition reduces element interactivity; backward chaining of the components builds the integration step by step.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 3.3 Step Down
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Different skill, simpler one, treated as a prerequisite to the original. The student leaves the original skill entirely, learns or re-learns the foundational concept that the original skill depended on, and only then returns to the original.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 When it works. 
\f2\b0 The loop has failed because the original skill is built on a prerequisite the student does not actually hold. No amount of work on the original skill will succeed because the foundation is missing. Step Down is the most expensive move \'97 it adds an entire teaching cycle on a different skill before returning \'97 but it is the only move that addresses the missing-foundation case.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 In practice. 
\f2\b0 For a chi-square interpretation problem the student keeps missing, Cramapple identifies that the underlying gap is the null hypothesis concept itself and routes the student to a short Score Loop on null hypothesis reasoning. Only after the student passes that returns the student to the original chi-square problem. For a Hardy-Weinberg problem, the prerequisite might be the algebraic manipulation rather than the population genetics. The diagnosis points at the prerequisite; the move runs a self-contained mini-loop on it.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 Source. 
\f2\b0 Bloom's mastery learning (1968, 1984) and VanLehn's review of intelligent tutoring systems (2011). The 2-sigma finding from Bloom's mastery-learning work is anchored in prerequisite tracing: when the loop fails, drop down to the foundational objective and confirm mastery there before retrying the higher-level objective. VanLehn's review finds the best human tutors and the best ITS converge on this move and achieve comparable effect sizes (~0.8\uc0\u963 ) precisely because of it.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa180\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf3 4. Selection Sequence
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The selection sequence is not diagnostic in the same way the Score Loop's Repair selection (LEARNING_SYSTEM 4.4) is diagnostic. The Score Loop infers an error type from the student's response and maps it to a Repair mode. The Stuck protocol does something different: it tries the cheapest move first, and where that fails, it hands the student the metacognitive choice between the two more expensive moves.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 4.1 First Move: Always Step Sideways
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 When a skill enters the Stuck protocol, Cramapple always begins with Step Sideways. This is the cheapest move in cognitive distance \'97 the student stays on the same skill at the same level. It also catches the largest single class of stuck-student cases, which is the misconception or surface-pattern failure that no amount of re-presentation will dislodge.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Starting with Step Sideways also matches the Score Loop's intra-loop escalation principle in Section 4.4 of LEARNING_SYSTEM: try the cheapest viable intervention first; escalate only when it fails. This is a structural consistency, not a coincidence \'97 both selection logics reflect the same commitment to minimal cognitive imposition consistent with closing the gap.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb120\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf2 Decision.
\f2\b0 \cf3  Step Sideways is always the first Stuck-protocol move on a skill, regardless of the diagnostic signal from the failed Score Loop attempts. The diagnostic step is used to select the contrasting case or reframing, not to select the move itself.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 4.2 If Sideways Resolves: Return to Score Loop
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 If the student successfully demonstrates the skill in the Sideways framing, Cramapple returns the skill to the Score Loop with the point credited. The Lock re-encounter is scheduled at the standard interval (24\'9672 hours per LEARNING_SYSTEM Section 5.1) using a surface form that does not duplicate the Sideways framing \'97 the Lock test must require transfer, not just recognition.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 4.3 If Sideways Does Not Resolve: Student-Choice Prompt
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 If the student misses the Sideways attempt, Cramapple does not silently pick the next move. It surfaces a choice to the student through a button-based prompt:
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs22 \cf3 \'93Let\'92s try this from a different angle. Would you like me to break this down into the component parts, or show you the foundational elements first?\'94
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Two buttons follow: Break it down (Step Apart) and Show me the foundation (Step Down). The student picks. The choice is offered, not assigned.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 Why student choice instead of Cramapple choosing. 
\f2\b0 The diagnostic signal at this point is too noisy to reliably distinguish a working-memory failure from a missing-foundation failure. More importantly, asking the student to reflect on which approach would help is itself a metacognitive skill worth building. Students who can answer \'93I need to see the pieces\'94 versus \'93I need to see the foundation\'94 are demonstrating self-knowledge about their own learning \'97 which transfers across subjects in a way the specific Stuck-protocol moves do not. The choice prompt converts a hard diagnostic call into a teaching moment.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \

\f0\b\fs22 \cf3 Why those two as the choices. 
\f2\b0 Step Apart and Step Down are the two more expensive moves; both have already failed by elimination to be the right next move (Sideways is gone). They also correspond to two distinct and recognizable kinds of stuckness \'97 \'93there\'92s too much going on\'94 and \'93I feel like I\'92m missing something underneath\'94 \'97 that students can map onto without specialized vocabulary.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 4.4 If the Chosen Move Does Not Resolve: Offer the Other
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 If the student's chosen move does not resolve the skill \'97 the student picks Step Apart and still misses on the recomposed attempt, or picks Step Down and still misses the original problem after the prerequisite is confirmed \'97 Cramapple offers the remaining move with a gentle reframe:
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs22 \cf3 \'93OK, let\'92s try the other approach. Want me to [show you the foundational elements / break this down into component parts] instead?\'94
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The second move is offered as a suggestion, not surfaced through another choice prompt. The student can decline and proceed to Park (Section 5) if they prefer.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb120\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf2 Decision.
\f2\b0 \cf3  Both Apart and Down are attempted before Park. Two moves' worth of attempts is enough signal to know whether the skill needs to come back later with fresh framing. One is not enough; three would push the student into frustration territory that the Park mechanism is explicitly designed to prevent.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 4.5 If Both Apart and Down Fail: Park
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 If the student has now run Sideways, then their chosen second move, then the alternate second move, and still missed the original skill \'97 Cramapple parks the skill. Park is detailed in Section 5. Briefly: the skill is removed from the current session, flagged in the Lock for re-emergence with fresh framing, and the student is given a graceful narrative that does not name failure.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

