\f0\b\fs32 \cf3 5. Park
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Park is the graceful exit from the Stuck protocol when all three moves have failed. It is the only move in Cramapple where the system explicitly stops trying to teach a specific skill in the current session. The decision to include Park at all is grounded in the mastery-learning literature's distinction between productive and unproductive struggle: when a skill has resisted three structurally different interventions, continuing to work on it produces frustration and disengagement, not learning.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 5.1 What Park Does
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa100\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f5\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Removes the skill from the current session. The student does not see another attempt on this skill in this sitting. Cramapple moves on to the next question or skill in the planned sequence.\
\ls2\ilvl0\ulnone \strike0\striked0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Flags the skill for content review. The skill is marked in Orly's content management system as having generated a three-move-no-resolution event for this student. Patterns across multiple students on the same skill are a signal that the underlying content (question, rubric, worked example, or prerequisite mapping) needs review. The flag is editorial, not student-visible.\
\ls2\ilvl0\ulnone \strike0\striked0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Returns the skill to the Lock with extended interval and fresh framing. The Lock surfaces the parked skill no sooner than 5\'967 days later (versus the standard 24\'9672 hours), and surfaces it with a different surface form than any used during the Stuck protocol. The extended interval is to allow the student's mental state to reset; the fresh framing is to avoid retriggering whatever surface pattern blocked the original loop.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\fs26 \cf3 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 5.2 What the Student Sees
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The student does not see the word \'93park\'94 and does not see anything that names failure. Cramapple presents a brief, gentle narrative:
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs22 \cf3 \'93This one\'92s tough. Let\'92s come back to it in a few days with fresh eyes \'97 I\'92ll bring it up again when you\'92re back. For now, let\'92s keep going.\'94
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Then Cramapple proceeds to the next item in the session. The student feels the encounter ending cleanly rather than grinding into frustration.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb120\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf2 Decision.
\f2\b0 \cf3  The framing is forward-looking (\'93come back in a few days\'94), not retrospective (\'93we tried three times\'94). The student does not need to know the protocol's mechanics to benefit from the protocol's exit.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 5.3 Re-Emergence
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs22 \cf3 When the parked skill resurfaces in the Lock after the extended interval, Cramapple treats the encounter as fresh \'97 the student re-enters the Score Loop on the skill at the normal Frame \uc0\u8594  Attempt \u8594  Score+Diagnose \u8594  Repair cadence. The skill is no longer flagged as stuck; the previous Stuck protocol attempts are not surfaced to the student.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Internally, Cramapple does retain the history. If the re-emergence attempt also fails into the three-miss trigger, the skill re-enters the Stuck protocol \'97 but now with the preference memory from Section 6 informing the choice prompt. If the re-emergence attempt is missed but does not trigger the protocol immediately, the skill simply re-enters the standard Lock cycle.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs18 \cf4 Open: number of Park re-emergence cycles before flagging the skill for editorial intervention. Working assumption: two parks on the same skill for the same student triggers a content-team review and a hold on further automated re-surfacing until a human (Orly or an authored content reviewer) inspects the question, rubric, and prerequisite mapping.
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa180\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf3 6. Lock Integration and Preference Memory
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The Lock (LEARNING_SYSTEM Section 5.1) is the spaced-retrieval queue. The Stuck protocol interacts with the Lock in three ways, all of which compound to make the protocol smarter about each individual student over time.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 6.1 Stuck-Resolved Skills
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 A skill that exits the Stuck protocol with resolution (passed Sideways, or passed Apart or Down) returns to the Lock at the standard interval but with a flag indicating it required Stuck-protocol intervention. The Lock surfaces it with deliberate non-similarity to the framing that resolved it \'97 if Step Sideways used a Mendelian cross to teach chi-square, the Lock re-encounter uses neither a Mendelian cross nor the original surface form. The point of the Lock is to test transfer, not to test recognition of the framing that worked.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs18 \cf4 Open: whether stuck-resolved skills should be Locked at a shorter interval than standard (e.g., 24 hours instead of 24\'9672 hours) given that the resolution was harder-won and is more fragile. Working assumption: same interval; revisit if usage data shows higher re-loss rates on stuck-resolved skills.
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 6.2 Stuck-Parked Skills
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 A parked skill returns to the Lock at the extended interval (5\'967 days, per Section 5.1). On re-encounter the skill is treated as a fresh Score Loop attempt. The parked-history flag is retained internally and is what triggers the preference memory (Section 6.3) if the skill enters the Stuck protocol a second time.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 6.3 Preference Memory
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Across encounters, Cramapple tracks which Stuck-protocol moves have produced resolution for each individual student. Three data points are tracked per student:
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa100\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f5\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Frequency: how many times the student has reached the choice prompt (after Sideways failed).\
\ls3\ilvl0\ulnone \strike0\striked0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Choice: which move the student picked each time \'97 Apart or Down.\
\ls3\ilvl0\ulnone \strike0\striked0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Outcome: whether the chosen move resolved the skill, whether the offered alternate resolved, or whether the skill was parked.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\i0\b0 \cf3 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 Once a student has reached the choice prompt three times and shown a consistent preference \'97 picking the same move two of three times and resolving with it more than half the time \'97 Cramapple biases the next choice prompt to lead with the preferred move. The prompt language stays identical; the button order swaps so the preferred move appears first. The student can still pick either.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb120\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf2 Decision.
\f2\b0 \cf3  Preference memory is a soft bias on button ordering, not a hard default. The choice is always still presented. The student retains agency. The preference memory is the mechanism by which the protocol's discovery framing \'97 \'93experiment with styles to find what unlocks learning\'94 \'97 pays off over time.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs18 \cf4 Open: thresholds. Three choice-prompt encounters before preference memory activates is a working assumption. The 2-of-3 consistency and 50% resolution threshold are working assumptions. All require calibration on real student data and may differ by student demographic or AP subject.
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 6.4 Cross-Student Aggregation
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Per LEARNING_SYSTEM Section 13.3, Cramapple does not train on student response data. But aggregate, anonymized signals about which Stuck-protocol move resolved which skills do inform content authoring. If chi-square consistently resolves through Step Down (null hypothesis prerequisite) and rarely through Step Apart, that is editorial signal for Orly: the chi-square content may need a prerequisite-strengthening pass. This use of aggregate data is consistent with the operating principle in LEARNING_SYSTEM 13.3 \'97 system-level improvement, not individual model training.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa180\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf3 7. UX Expression
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 The Stuck protocol's UX commitment is soft visibility: the student notices that Cramapple's approach has shifted, but does not see anything that names the student as failing, stuck, or struggling. The shape difference between the Score Loop and the Stuck protocol is the signal; explicit labeling is not.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 7.1 What the Student Sees on Entry
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 When a skill enters the Stuck protocol, the next interaction begins with a brief reframe rather than another Score Loop iteration:
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs22 \cf3 \'93Let me approach this from a different angle.\'94
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 That line, followed by the Step Sideways framing, is the only marker. The student is not told \'93you\'92ve missed this three times\'94 or \'93you\'92re struggling.\'94 The shift in framing carries the meaning.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 7.2 The Choice Prompt
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 When Sideways fails, the choice prompt is delivered as inline text followed by two buttons. The button-based form factor is the right primitive for this interaction for three reasons. It is fast \'97 one tap rather than a typed response. It is concrete \'97 the student picks between two labeled options rather than generating an open answer. And it constrains the response space \'97 there is no possibility of misunderstanding what the student meant, which matters for a metacognitive prompt where ambiguity would defeat the purpose.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb120\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs22 \cf2 Decision.
\f2\b0 \cf3  Choice prompt: two buttons. Button labels: \'93Break it down\'94 (Step Apart) and \'93Show me the foundation\'94 (Step Down). Order: Apart left, Down right by default. Preference memory may swap the order per Section 6.3.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs18 \cf4 Open: visual treatment of the buttons relative to the rest of the question screen. The button-based prompt should be clearly distinct from MCQ choices (which are also buttons) to avoid the student reading the choice prompt as part of the question. Working assumption: the prompt renders in a contained block with a subtle background color shift, positioned below the question region. UX details to be specified in the design system once the broader landing-page and Score-panel patterns are locked.
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 7.3 What the Student Does Not See
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa100\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f5\i\b\fs22 \cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 The word \'93stuck.\'94\
\ls4\ilvl0\ulnone \strike0\striked0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Counters or progress indicators showing how many attempts have failed.\
\ls4\ilvl0\ulnone \strike0\striked0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 References to other students or aggregate difficulty data.\
\ls4\ilvl0\ulnone \strike0\striked0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Explanations of which move is being run or why.\
\ls4\ilvl0\ulnone \strike0\striked0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\ul \strike \strikec3 Any acknowledgment that the protocol exists as a distinct mode from the Score Loop.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\i0\b0 \cf3 \ulnone \strike0\striked0 These are deliberate omissions. The product's commitment is that the student experiences a coherent teacher who is adjusting to them, not a state machine that has flagged them as off-track. Naming the mode would convert a teaching moment into a diagnostic moment, which violates the soft-visibility commitment.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa140\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs26 \cf3 7.4 Parent and Effort Surfaces
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Per LEARNING_SYSTEM Section 10.5 and Section 12 (Open Items), the parent-facing effort rollup is still being designed. The Stuck protocol contributes one signal to that surface: the number of skills currently parked. The wording surfaced to parents follows the same soft-visibility principle \'97 \'93skills set aside for a fresh look later this week\'94 rather than \'93skills the student has failed.\'94 Parents see a forward-looking aggregate; they do not see the per-attempt data.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs18 \cf4 Open: whether the parent surface shows the parked-skill count at all, or only the aggregate effort metric. Tied to the broader open item on parent-facing rollup design in LEARNING_SYSTEM Section 12.
\f1\i0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb360\sa180\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf3 8. Research and Source Linkage
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf3 Each Stuck-protocol move and the Park exit traces to a specific research base, consistent with the methodology premise in LEARNING_SYSTEM Section 1 that every teaching activity should be traceable to either cognitive science, College Board guidance, or documented AP teacher practice. The Stuck protocol is grounded in the cognitive-science branch of that triad; College Board materials do not speak directly to remediation.
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
